WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.150
- [Reporter] Concerns that a
new 5G service could pose risks

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:06.310
to aircraft instruments has
prompted international airlines

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.110
to cancel some US-bound flights,

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:11.510
even after AT&amp;T and Verizon
agreed to limit the signal

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.000
near US airports.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.170
The pace of cancellations eased Wednesday,

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.170
once it became clear

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.323
that the FAA would allow
most flights to proceed.

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.270
Airlines have been caught
in the middle of a dispute

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:24.690
between the wireless industry

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.860
and the Federal Aviation Administration

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.330
and executives have
repeatedly raised alarms

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.190
about what this could mean for business.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:32.980
- I think if you were to ask us

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.690
what our number one concern
is here in the near term,

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:38.140
it is the deployment of 5G.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.610
- [Reporter] Airline
executives have warned

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.280
of significant flight disruptions

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.080
after the FAA said it
could impose restrictions

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.240
on landing aircraft

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.090
in up to 46 of the country's
largest metropolitan areas

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.660
where the service was
scheduled to be rolled out.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.270
The FAA says the new 5G signals
could possibly interfere

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:57.100
with cockpit safety systems,

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.100
but the wireless industry is pushing back

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.510
against those claims.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:01.960
Here's what you need to know.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.270
A bulletin sent by the FAA

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:07.640
says that 5G interference could result

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.227
in "erroneous altimeter readings

00:01:09.227 --> 00:01:11.810
"and loss of altimeter function".

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:15.070
A radar, or radio altimeter,
is a key piece of equipment

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.480
that tells a pilot the plane's altitude.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.720
It does this by sending
radio waves to the ground

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.380
that return to the aircraft.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.250
Radar altimeters send
signals at narrow frequencies

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.800
that wouldn't interfere
with the planned 5G signal,

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.380
but some were sensitive
models could receive signals

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.570
that come from the cellular towers.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.500
The FAA says this has the potential

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.460
to produce inaccurate readings.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:36.960
- If they can't be relied on

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.980
because of potential interference,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.900
then the regulators are
going to prohibit use

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.160
of certain cockpit safety systems

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.980
that rely on these devices,

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.830
and that's gonna have
significant ripple effects

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.300
for air travel.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.290
- [Reporter] Airplane manufacturers

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.960
have also raised a red flag
about 5G's impact on safety.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.470
- The aerospace
manufacturers are concerned

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:02.710
because they don't want
their planes to crash.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:06.340
They have a keen interest in making sure

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:10.160
that airlines can safely
operate their products.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.560
- [Reporter] The CEOs of Boeing

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.480
and Airbus Americas sent a letter

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.640
to Transportation Secretary
Pete Buttigieg in December.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:17.747
The executives said

00:02:17.747 --> 00:02:20.327
"5G interference could
adversely affect the ability

00:02:20.327 --> 00:02:22.160
"of aircraft to safely operate"

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.037
and that FAA restrictions
as a result could have

00:02:25.037 --> 00:02:28.120
"an enormous negative impact
on the aviation industry".

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.763
But not everyone agrees
with the safety concerns.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.580
The Federal Communications Commission

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.140
said that the limits it had
set on the cellular service

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.428
would be enough to protect air traffic.

00:02:38.428 --> 00:02:41.300
- They've got spectrum
experts and engineers

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:45.210
that don't see any risks
to aviation safety.

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:48.300
- [Reporter] And CTIA, a
wireless industry trade group,

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.160
has responded sharply to the concerns

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:51.820
voiced by the FAA and others,

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.877
saying "the aviation industry's
fear-mongering relies

00:02:54.877 --> 00:02:56.757
"on completely discredited information

00:02:56.757 --> 00:02:59.440
"and deliberate distortions of fact".

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.210
The industry group also
points to the frequencies used

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:03.870
in nearly 40 countries

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.490
and says none has reported
any harmful interference

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.660
with aviation equipment, but-

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.210
- What may work in one country
may not work in another

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:16.090
because 5G is rolled out
in a different spectrum

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:17.840
or at different power levels,

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.200
with antennas pointed
in different directions.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.690
Some countries have imposed
buffer zones around airports.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:28.480
It's really not apples to apples.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.070
- [Reporter] Airlines
have urged cooperation

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:32.010
with the wireless industry and regulators

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:33.100
to resolve the issue

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.540
to avoid disruptions to their operations.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:39.070
- I do know that the safety concerns

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:41.890
with aircraft and aviation are very real,

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:44.200
and I also know that
there's a solution here.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.980
We could absolutely solve this

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:49.815
and live in a world where
there is 5G available.

00:03:49.815 --> 00:03:51.427
- [Reporter] AT&amp;T and
Verizon had been planning

00:03:51.427 --> 00:03:54.530
to switch on the new
service on January 5th,

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:56.570
but reversed course on January 3rd.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:03:58.910
The wireless carriers
agreed to delay the rollout

00:03:58.910 --> 00:03:59.743
by two weeks

00:03:59.743 --> 00:04:02.320
at the request of the
Transportation Department.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:03.650
- The Transportation Department

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:07.030
and the FAA were essentially
asking for more time

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:08.450
so they can figure out

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.760
how to narrowly tailor
flight restrictions.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:12.890
- [Reporter] During those two weeks,

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.960
the FAA picked 50 airports

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.910
where buffer zones would be implemented,

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:19.180
limiting 5G signals for six months

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:20.470
to help avoid disruptions

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.350
from the expected flight restrictions.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.060
But Verizon and AT&amp;T agreed

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:25.900
to limit the service even further,

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.730
saying they will not switch the service on

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:30.050
within two miles of airport runways.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:31.410
Aviation safety officials

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:32.970
say these limits will avert many,

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.957
but likely not all,
cancellations and delays.

00:04:35.957 --> 00:04:38.220
- We are ready for you to
send us a route revision.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.740
- [Reporter] The FAA says it will work

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.890
with airplane manufacturers and airlines

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:44.040
to ensure aircraft can safely operate

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.710
with the new cell service.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:47.840
The agency says it would ease restrictions

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.770
as more altimeters are tested

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.295
and either deemed safe,
retrofitted, or replaced.

00:04:53.295 --> 00:04:55.962
(pensive music)

